    "narrative": "For our May 5th slate, the lineup is anchored by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (71.77) and **Bryson Stott** (70.68), both of whom benefit from highly favorable matchups against back-end rotation arms. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** draws Kolek, and despite a slight hit for the risk of limited steal opportunities, his ability to capitalize on a 4.39 xERA starter makes him an essential start. Meanwhile, **Bryson Stott** surges to a 70.68 efficiency score; he is our top play of the day due to a massive +13% platoon edge against Severino and a +14% benefit from the opposing pitcher\u2019s skill profile. Elsewhere, the matchup against Buehler provides a high-ceiling environment for **Matt Chapman** (66.68) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (65.65), as both benefit from Buehler\u2019s high-xERA vulnerability and a specific 90% steal-rate advantage tied to his slow delivery.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today's roster involved **Gavin Sheets**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 41.99 score. While **Gavin Sheets** received a boost from his batting order slot, the sheer difficulty of facing Logan Webb\u2014a high-end arm with a 3.30 xERA\u2014rendered him a liability compared to the superior platoon matchups we have elsewhere in the outfield. As we look at the broader landscape, stay wary of the rainout risk affecting **Tyler Freeman**, who was excluded from consideration entirely due to the postponement. While the team is well-positioned to chase counting stats, keep an eye on **Daulton Varsho**; despite a marginal score of 50.85, the 100% stolen base environment against Rasmussen keeps him relevant if you are desperate to bridge the gap in our scarcest category.",
